17
00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:54.760
But how did the model learn to do this?

18
00:00:55.281 --> 00:00:59.610
The main tool used to train an LLM
is actually supervised learning.

19
00:01:00.792 --> 00:01:03.628
In supervised learning,
a computer learns an input-output

20
00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:07.174
or X or Y mapping
using labeled training data. So

21
00:01:07.174 --> 00:01:10.010
for example, if you're using
supervised learning to

22
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.555
learn to classify the sentiment of
restaurant reviews, you might

23
00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:16.391
collect a training set
like this, where a

24
00:01:16.391 --> 00:01:19.581
review like, "The pastrami
sandwich is great!", is labeled

25
00:01:19.581 --> 00:01:22.417
as a positive sentiment review, and
so on.

26
00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:25.896
And "Service was slow,
the food was so-so.", it

27
00:01:25.896 --> 00:01:28.893
was negative, and "The
earl grey tea was fantastic.", has

28
00:01:28.893 --> 00:01:29.792
a positive label.

29
00:01:30.414 --> 00:01:33.037
By the way,
both Isa and I were born in the UK,

30
00:01:33.037 --> 00:01:35.223
and so both of us
like our earl grey tea.

31
00:01:35.905 --> 00:01:38.775
And so the process
for supervised learning is

32
00:01:38.775 --> 00:01:41.645
typically to get labeled
data and then train

33
00:01:41.645 --> 00:01:43.080
AI model on data.

34
00:01:43.641 --> 00:01:45.593
And after training,
you can then deploy

35
00:01:45.593 --> 00:01:48.940
and call the model and give it a
new restaurant review like

36
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.335
best pizza I've ever had.

37
00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:53.401
You hopefully output that
as a positive sentiment.

38
00:01:54.323 --> 00:01:57.139
It turns out that
supervised learning is a

39
00:01:57.139 --> 00:01:59.954
core building block for
training Large Language Models.

40
00:02:00.656 --> 00:02:04.319
Specifically, a Large Language
Model can be built by using

41
00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:07.249
supervised learning to
repeatedly predict the next word.

42
00:02:07.991 --> 00:02:12.210
Let's say that in your training sets
of a lot of text data, you

43
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.922
have to sentence, "My
favorite food is a bagel

44
00:02:14.922 --> 00:02:16.428
with cream cheese and lox.".

45
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.534
Then this sentence is turned into
a sequence of training examples,

46
00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:25.355
where given a sentence fragment,
"My favorite food is a",

47
00:02:25.355 --> 00:02:30.322
if you want to predict the
next word in this case was "bagel",

48
00:02:30.322 --> 00:02:33.379
or given the sentence
fragment or sentence prefix,

49
00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.729
"My favorite food is a bagel",
the next word in this case would be

50
00:02:38.729 --> 00:02:40.258
"with", and so on.

51
00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:43.801
And given a large training set
of hundreds of

52
00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:46.847
billions or sometimes
even more words, you can then create

53
00:02:46.847 --> 00:02:49.284
a massive training set
where you can start

54
00:02:49.284 --> 00:02:52.940
off with part of a sentence or part
of a piece of

55
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.377
text and repeatedly ask
the language model to

56
00:02:55.377 --> 00:02:57.814
learn to predict what
is the next word.

57
00:02:58.395 --> 00:03:01.677
So today there are
broadly two major types

58
00:03:01.677 --> 00:03:05.779
of Large Language Models.
The first is a "Base LLM"

59
00:03:05.779 --> 00:03:09.471
and the second,
which is what is increasingly used,

60
00:03:09.471 --> 00:03:11.522
is the "Instruction Tuned LLM".

61
00:03:12.464 --> 00:03:14.599
So the base LLM
repeatedly predicts the next

62
00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.336
word based on text training data.
And so if I give it a prompt,

63
00:03:18.336 --> 00:03:21.272
"Once upon a time there was a unicorn",
then it may,

64
00:03:21.272 --> 00:03:24.474
by repeatedly predicting one
word at a time, come up with a

65
00:03:24.474 --> 00:03:26.610
completion that tells a
story about a unicorn

66
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.279
living in a magical
forest with all her unicorn friends.

67
00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:29.279


68
00:03:30.461 --> 00:03:34.337
Now, a downside of this is that
if you were to prompt it with

69
00:03:34.337 --> 00:03:37.105
"What is the capital of France?",
quite possible that on

70
00:03:37.105 --> 00:03:39.319
the internet there might
be a list of

71
00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:42.088
quiz questions about France.
So it may complete this with

72
00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:45.133
"What is France's largest city, what
is France's population?", and so

73
00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:45.410
on.

74
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.502
But what you really
want is you want it to tell you

75
00:03:49.502 --> 00:03:51.521
what is the capital of France,
probably,

76
00:03:51.521 --> 00:03:53.252
rather than list all these questions.


77
00:03:54.314 --> 00:03:57.336
So an Instruction Tuned LLM instead
tries to follow

78
00:03:57.336 --> 00:04:01.029
instructions and will hopefully say,
"The capital of France is Paris.".

79
00:04:02.091 --> 00:04:05.930
How do you go from a
Base LLM to an Instruction Tuned

80
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.770
LLM? This is what the process of
training an Instruction Tuned LLM,

81
00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.050
like ChatGPT, looks like.

82
00:04:11.691 --> 00:04:15.024
You first train a Base
LLM on a lot of data,

83
00:04:15.024 --> 00:04:18.963
so hundreds of billions of words,
maybe even more. And this is a

84
00:04:18.963 --> 00:04:21.387
process that can take
months on a large

85
00:04:21.387 --> 00:04:21.993
supercomputing system.

86
00:04:23.055 --> 00:04:27.472
After you've trained the Base LLM, you
would then further train

87
00:04:27.472 --> 00:04:32.692
the model by fine-tuning it
on a smaller set of examples, where the

88
00:04:32.692 --> 00:04:37.110
output follows an input instruction. And
so, for example, you may

89
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.929
have contractors help you
write a lot of examples of an instruction,

90
00:04:41.929 --> 00:04:45.141
and then a good response to
an instruction.

91
00:04:45.642 --> 00:04:48.467
And that creates a
training set to carry

92
00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:52.351
out this additional fine-tuning. So
that learns to predict what is

93
00:04:52.351 --> 00:04:56.588
the next word if it's trying
to follow an instruction. After that,

94
00:04:56.588 --> 00:04:59.766
to improve
the quality of the LLM's output, a

95
00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:04.709
common process now is to obtain human ratings
of the quality of many different

96
00:05:04.709 --> 00:05:07.181
LLM outputs on criteria, such as
whether

97
00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:09.653
the output is helpful, honest, and harmless.


98
00:05:10.514 --> 00:05:13.387
And you can then
further tune the LLM

99
00:05:13.387 --> 00:05:16.259
to increase the probability
of its generating the

100
00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:19.490
more highly rated outputs.
And the most common technique

101
00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:23.439
to do this is RLHF, which stands
for Reinforcement Learning from

102
00:05:23.439 --> 00:05:25.593
Human Feedback.
And whereas training the

103
00:05:25.593 --> 00:05:28.825
Base LLM can take months,
the process of going

104
00:05:28.825 --> 00:05:31.697
from the Base LLM
to the Instruction Tuned

105
00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:37.082
LLM can be done in maybe days on
a much more modest size data sets,

106
00:05:37.082 --> 00:05:39.595
and much more
modest size computational resources.

107
00:05:39.595 --> 00:05:39.595


