120
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:37.487
Now, in the description of the
Large Language Model so far,

121
00:06:37.487 --> 00:06:41.124
I talked about it as
predicting one word at a time,

122
00:06:41.124 --> 00:06:43.769
but there's actually one more
important technical detail.

123
00:06:44.551 --> 00:06:49.396
If you were to tell it,
take the letters in the word lollipop,

124
00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:52.751
and reverse them,
this seems like an easy task,

125
00:06:52.751 --> 00:06:55.733
maybe like a four-year-old could
do this task.

126
00:06:56.514 --> 00:07:00.683
But if you were to
ask ChatGPT to do this,

127
00:07:00.683 --> 00:07:04.435
it actually outputs a
somewhat garbled whatever this is.

128
00:07:04.435 --> 00:07:10.271
This is not L-O-L-I-P-O-P, this is not lollipop's letters reversed.
So why is ChatGPT

129
00:07:10.271 --> 00:07:14.857
unable to do
what seems like a relatively simple task? It

130
00:07:14.857 --> 00:07:19.442
turns out that there's one more
important detail for how a

131
00:07:19.442 --> 00:07:22.777
Large Language Model works,
which is it doesn't

132
00:07:22.777 --> 00:07:26.112
actually repeatedly predict the
next word, it instead

133
00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:28.197
repeatedly predicts the
next token.

134
00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:28.197



135
00:07:28.758 --> 00:07:34.256
And what an LLM actually does is it
will take a sequence of characters,

136
00:07:34.256 --> 00:07:37.791
like "Learning new things is fun!",
and group the

137
00:07:37.791 --> 00:07:40.148
characters together to
form tokens that

138
00:07:40.148 --> 00:07:42.505
comprise commonly occurring
sequences of characters.

139
00:07:43.186 --> 00:07:47.950
So here, learning new things is fun,
each of them is a fairly common word,

140
00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.491
and so each token
corresponds to one word,

141
00:07:50.491 --> 00:07:53.667
or one word in a space,
or an exclamation mark.

142
00:07:54.548 --> 00:07:58.277
But if you were to
give it input with some

143
00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:00.514
somewhat less frequently used words,
like

144
00:08:00.514 --> 00:08:03.869
"Prompting as powerful developer tool.",
the word prompting is

145
00:08:03.869 --> 00:08:06.852
still not that common
in the English language,

146
00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:08.716
but certainly gaining in popularity.


147
00:08:09.438 --> 00:08:14.377
And so prompting is actually broken
down to three tokens with "'prom", "pt",

148
00:08:14.377 --> 00:08:17.416
and "ing", because those three
are commonly occurring

149
00:08:17.416 --> 00:08:18.556
sequences of letters.

150
00:08:19.417 --> 00:08:22.707
And if you were to
give it the word lollipop,

151
00:08:22.707 --> 00:08:25.997
the tokenizer actually breaks
this down into three tokens, "l"

152
00:08:25.997 --> 00:08:27.313
and "oll" and "ipop".

153
00:08:28.034 --> 00:08:31.706
And because ChatGPT isn't
seeing the individual letters, is

154
00:08:31.706 --> 00:08:36.193
instead seeing these three tokens, it's
more difficult for it to

155
00:08:36.193 --> 00:08:39.457
correctly print out these
letters in reverse order.

156
00:08:39.457 --> 00:08:39.457


157
00:08:40.038 --> 00:08:43.765
So here's
a trick you can use to fix this.

158
00:08:44.647 --> 00:08:47.693
If I were to add
dashes to the word dashes,

159
00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:50.130
between these letters,
and spaces would work too,

160
00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:54.395
or other things would work too,
and tell it to take the letters and

161
00:08:54.395 --> 00:08:56.831
lollipop and reverse them,
then it actually does

162
00:08:56.831 --> 00:08:58.659
a much better job, this L-O-L-L-I-P-O-P.


163
00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.557
And the reason for that is,
if you pass it lollipop

164
00:09:02.557 --> 00:09:05.336
with dashes in between the letters,
it tokenizes each

165
00:09:05.336 --> 00:09:07.498
of these characters
into an individual token,

166
00:09:07.498 --> 00:09:11.203
making it easier for it to see the
individual letters and print

167
00:09:11.203 --> 00:09:12.747
them out in reverse order.

168
00:09:13.789 --> 00:09:18.272
So if you ever want to
use ChatGPT to play a word game,

169
00:09:18.272 --> 00:09:21.721
like word or scrabble or something,
this nifty trick helps

170
00:09:21.721 --> 00:09:25.170
it to better see
the individual letters of the words.

171
00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:25.170


172
00:09:26.114 --> 00:09:29.077
For the English language,
one token roughly on average,

173
00:09:29.077 --> 00:09:31.711
corresponds to about four
characters or about three

174
00:09:31.711 --> 00:09:33.027
quarters of a word.

175
00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:36.290
And so different
Large Language Models

176
00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:40.690
will often have different limits on
the number of input plus output

177
00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:44.723
tokens it can accept.
The input is often called the context,

178
00:09:44.723 --> 00:09:47.656
and the output is
often called the completion.

179
00:09:47.656 --> 00:09:50.956
And the model GPT 3.5 Turbo, for
example, the

180
00:09:50.956 --> 00:09:55.723
most commonly used chat GPT model, has
a limit of roughly 4,000 tokens

181
00:09:55.723 --> 00:09:57.556
in the input plus output.


182
00:09:58.318 --> 00:10:02.094
So if you try to
feed it an input context that's

183
00:10:02.094 --> 00:10:05.526
much longer than this, it'll actually
throw an exception or

184
00:10:05.526 --> 00:10:08.958
generate an error. Next, I
want to share with you

